      "text": "First, we analyze the given inequality:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x+1} \\ge \\frac{2}{x-1} + \\frac{5}{4}.\n\\]\nStep 1: Simplify the right-hand side\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{x-1} + \\frac{5}{4} = \\frac{8 + 5(x-1)}{4(x-1)} = \\frac{5x + 3}{4(x-1)}.\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Move all terms to the left-hand side\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x+1} - \\frac{5x+3}{4(x-1)} \\ge 0.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Find a common denominator and combine the fractions\n\\[\n\\frac{4x^2 - (5x+3)(x+1)}{4(x+1)(x-1)} \\ge 0,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 - 5x^2 - x - 15}{4(x+1)(x-1)} \\ge 0,\n\\]\nwhich further simplifies to\n\\[\n\\frac{-4x^2 - x - 15}{4(x+1)(x-1)} \\ge 0,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + x + 4}{4(x+1)(x-1)} \\le 0.\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Factor and determine intervals\nThe numerator has no real roots, so there is no commonality between the roots and the denominators. Therefore, we only need to determine where the numerator, $x^2 + x + 4$, is negative. We observe that $x^2 + x + 4$ has a negative leading coefficient and a discriminant of $1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 4 = 1 - 16 = -15$, which is negative. Thus, $x^2 + x + 4$ is always positive. Due to this fact, the fraction $\\frac{x^2 + x + 4}{4(x+1)(x-1)}$ is always positive, leading to an inequality that never holds true.\n\nHence, no $x$ satisfies the original inequality. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{No solution}}\\).",
